    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tRobert and Marianne Huber, Dixon, IL [1]; 1969, sold to D. Daniel Michel, Chicago (no. 69:153) [2]; 1991, sold through Ancient Art of the New World, New York to US private collection [3]; October 5, 2020, sold through Arte Primitivo, New York, lot 112, to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] According to US private collection.\n\t<br />\n\t[2] Michel assigned acquisition numbers to each of the objects in his collection, with the first digits indicating the year of purchase, in this case 69 for 1969. This has been supported by examination of other objects in his collection that have been published and exhibited.\n\t<br />\n\t[3] According to US private collection.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Animals frequently grace the interiors of Los Tuxtlas bowls, with birds, snakes, and monkeys all common, but only three are known to depict deer. Although the figure is highly stylized, with a sausage-like body, its white underbelly and tail suggest it is a white-tailed deer. Volutes in white and red extend from the deer’s nose and ear while a red projection with clustered white dots flows beneath the belly. The lolling tongue and red flow from the belly may indicate a sacrificed animal or one hunted, perhaps to be eaten from this very vessel. The colorful palette and several aspects of the geometric designs adorning the vessel’s walls are similar to designs in contemporaneous Maya plates, likely reflecting increased interaction between the Maya and coastal Veracruz cultures during the so-called Epiclassic period.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
